WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.934 --> 00:00:02.035
-Our first guest tonight is

00:00:02.035 --> 00:00:03.970
an Emmy and Tony Award-winning
actress

00:00:03.970 --> 00:00:05.839
you know from her work
on "The Good Wife,"

00:00:05.839 --> 00:00:08.408
"The Gilded Age,"
and the "Mamma Mia" movies.

00:00:08.408 --> 00:00:11.110
She stars in the sixth,
and final, season

00:00:11.110 --> 00:00:12.946
of "The Good Fight"
which premiers this Thursday

00:00:12.946 --> 00:00:15.882
on Paramount+.
Let's take a look.

00:00:15.882 --> 00:00:18.417
-I really don't know
why I'm here. [ Chuckle ]

00:00:18.417 --> 00:00:23.690
Well, I think my world feels
a little --

00:00:23.690 --> 00:00:26.226
a little bit out of control.

00:00:26.226 --> 00:00:27.827
I feel like we're
on this hamster wheel.

00:00:27.827 --> 00:00:29.862
No matter what we do, we just

00:00:29.862 --> 00:00:32.665
end up back at the start,
you know?

00:00:32.665 --> 00:00:34.700
I used to believe in progress,
that --

00:00:34.700 --> 00:00:38.037
that...people learn
from their mistakes,

00:00:38.037 --> 00:00:40.807
that things got better, and --

00:00:40.807 --> 00:00:41.740
and here we are.

00:00:43.008 --> 00:00:44.977
-Please welcome back to the show
one of our favorites --

00:00:44.977 --> 00:00:46.812
Christine Baranski, everybody!

00:00:46.812 --> 00:00:48.814
[ Cheering and applause ]

00:00:48.814 --> 00:00:50.817
[ Funk plays ]

00:00:50.817 --> 00:00:59.425 line:0%
♪♪

00:00:59.425 --> 00:01:04.530 line:0%
♪♪

00:01:04.530 --> 00:01:06.232
-How are you?!

00:01:08.734 --> 00:01:10.469
-I know.
Back from summer vacation.

00:01:10.469 --> 00:01:12.638
-Back from summer vacation.
You're a working actress.

00:01:12.638 --> 00:01:14.674
I feel like you don't really
have your summers off.

00:01:14.674 --> 00:01:16.308
-I didn't, not this summer.

00:01:16.308 --> 00:01:20.846
I worked "Good Fight"
through July

00:01:20.846 --> 00:01:22.848
and then I had a day off

00:01:22.848 --> 00:01:24.417
and then I went right
into "Gilded Age,"

00:01:24.417 --> 00:01:26.051
but I've had a little bit
of time.

00:01:26.051 --> 00:01:28.488
-Well, I was very lucky in the
early parts of the summer.

00:01:28.488 --> 00:01:31.123
We've talked in the past
about that fact that our --

00:01:31.123 --> 00:01:33.626
my children, and your
grandchildren are very close.

00:01:33.626 --> 00:01:35.794
-I know, the last time
I saw you, really,

00:01:35.794 --> 00:01:40.133
you were mounting an inflatable
water trampoline.

00:01:40.133 --> 00:01:41.334
[ Laughter ]
-I was.

00:01:41.334 --> 00:01:42.601
-I wish I had a video of it.

00:01:42.601 --> 00:01:45.638
-I'm glad you don't have
a video of me,

00:01:45.638 --> 00:01:46.706
[ Laughter ]
but you brought one

00:01:46.706 --> 00:01:48.975
of your grandson.
-I did.

00:01:48.975 --> 00:01:50.309
-Do you want
to set up the video?

00:01:50.309 --> 00:01:52.478
And let me just say,
I really appreciate,

00:01:52.478 --> 00:01:54.347
as a grandmother, that you're
going to share it with us.

00:01:54.347 --> 00:01:55.781
[ Laughter ]

00:01:55.781 --> 00:01:57.916
-Well, here's the thing.
We have a long dock

00:01:57.916 --> 00:01:59.652
and then we have
a water trampoline

00:01:59.652 --> 00:02:02.055
and the water trampoline
kind of floats away.

00:02:02.055 --> 00:02:04.657
Well, Christopher,
my son-in-law,

00:02:04.657 --> 00:02:07.226
jumped from the dock
onto the water trampoline

00:02:07.226 --> 00:02:10.029
and then Max, my oldest one,
my eight-year-old,

00:02:10.029 --> 00:02:12.298
went from the dock
onto the water trampoline.

00:02:12.298 --> 00:02:14.466
But Auggie --
-Yeah.

00:02:14.466 --> 00:02:15.801
-Yeah, here's Auggie.

00:02:15.801 --> 00:02:17.937
[ Laughter ]
-I think you've set it up
very well.

00:02:17.937 --> 00:02:20.006
Let's take a look at Auggie
trying to do the same.

00:02:21.440 --> 00:02:22.274
-Ah!

00:02:22.274 --> 00:02:22.975
-Oh!

00:02:22.975 --> 00:02:25.645
[ Laughter and applause ]

00:02:27.614 --> 00:02:28.747
-He's okay.

00:02:28.747 --> 00:02:30.182
-He's fine.
-He's fine.

00:02:30.182 --> 00:02:31.917
-I mean, he's not going to be
fine when he finds out

00:02:31.917 --> 00:02:34.120
that his grandmother showed that
on national television.

00:02:34.120 --> 00:02:35.788
-No, actually, I asked him.

00:02:35.788 --> 00:02:37.390
-Did you really?
-He's very excited.

00:02:37.390 --> 00:02:38.491
He's very excited.
-That's great.

00:02:38.491 --> 00:02:39.792
[ Laughter ]
I mean, I will say,

00:02:39.792 --> 00:02:42.695
my experience with Auggie is
he's very robust.

00:02:42.695 --> 00:02:44.897
I am not surprised that he was
uninjured by that.

00:02:44.897 --> 00:02:49.302
-No. But can you imagine needing
rhinoplasty in first grade?

00:02:49.302 --> 00:02:50.336
[ Laughter ]

00:02:50.336 --> 00:02:51.837
I was delighted.

00:02:51.837 --> 00:02:54.707
It's a very funny memory.

00:02:54.707 --> 00:02:56.775
-So, my kids know you as Minnie,

00:02:56.775 --> 00:02:58.610
which is what your grandchildren
call you.

00:02:58.610 --> 00:03:02.014
-Everybody, everybody at the
beach, I'm just Minnie.

00:03:02.014 --> 00:03:03.215
-You're just Minnie.
It's great.

00:03:03.215 --> 00:03:05.218
-I love and then they hug me,
"Bye, Minnie,"

00:03:05.218 --> 00:03:06.586
and "When are we going
to Minnie's house?"

00:03:06.586 --> 00:03:08.821
It's something
that I'm very proud of,

00:03:08.821 --> 00:03:11.257
that kids like to come
to Minnie's house.

00:03:11.257 --> 00:03:13.125
And you like it there, too,
don't you?

00:03:13.125 --> 00:03:15.094
-I do like it there
a great deal.

00:03:15.094 --> 00:03:17.229
I mean, now I'm a little worried
about my privacy

00:03:17.229 --> 00:03:20.733
because you share videos.
[ Laughter ]

00:03:20.733 --> 00:03:24.503
-My favorite was watching adults
try and mount the trampoline

00:03:24.503 --> 00:03:26.472
and then drag their kids out.

00:03:26.472 --> 00:03:28.408
-Yes, I actually
don't blame Auggie

00:03:28.408 --> 00:03:30.509
for trying to jump on,
because it's not as easy

00:03:30.509 --> 00:03:32.544
crawling up the side ladder
as you look.

00:03:32.544 --> 00:03:35.247
I want to ask you --
So, we had a moment.

00:03:35.247 --> 00:03:37.450
We were listening
to the "Mamma Mia!" soundtrack,

00:03:37.450 --> 00:03:39.418
as we do,
and we realized

00:03:39.418 --> 00:03:42.622
we could tell the boys,
"Oh, that's Minnie's voice."

00:03:42.622 --> 00:03:44.691
And it did not register
with them at all.

00:03:44.691 --> 00:03:47.093
It was too big a leap
for their logic to make.

00:03:47.093 --> 00:03:49.128
-Do you want me to
make a house call

00:03:49.128 --> 00:03:50.797
and sing all the song.
-We would love you to come over.

00:03:50.797 --> 00:03:51.997
[ Laughter ]

00:03:51.997 --> 00:03:54.266
-♪ You can dance ♪

00:03:54.266 --> 00:03:56.035
♪ You can dance ♪

00:03:56.035 --> 00:03:58.771
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:03:58.771 --> 00:04:00.272
-Do your...

00:04:00.272 --> 00:04:02.241
I mean, you know,
most of your work

00:04:02.241 --> 00:04:04.243
is obviously, I would imagine,
fairly adult.

00:04:04.243 --> 00:04:06.812
Do your grandkids --
have they made the connection

00:04:06.812 --> 00:04:08.280
that you are on-screen?

00:04:08.280 --> 00:04:11.718
-They're curiously
so uninterested.

00:04:11.718 --> 00:04:13.152
They don't get all excited.

00:04:13.152 --> 00:04:14.586
They watch "The Grinch,"
and it's like,

00:04:14.586 --> 00:04:16.388
"Oh, yeah, that's Minnie."

00:04:16.388 --> 00:04:19.558
But other kids come up,
"Oh! Oh, you're in 'Mamma Mia!'"

00:04:19.558 --> 00:04:20.860
"Oh, you're Martha May
in "The Grinch."

00:04:20.860 --> 00:04:23.395
And my own grand--
which is fine.

00:04:23.395 --> 00:04:24.730
-Yeah.
-I actually don't want to be

00:04:24.730 --> 00:04:27.800
a famous person to them.
-You want to be Minnie.

00:04:27.800 --> 00:04:30.036
And you're an excellent Minnie.
You can do both.

00:04:30.036 --> 00:04:34.240
Speaking of doing both,
you are playing two characters

00:04:34.240 --> 00:04:36.976
about 100 years apart
in American history.

00:04:36.976 --> 00:04:39.545
-Both of them in despair
about the state of the world.

00:04:39.545 --> 00:04:41.547
-Exactly.
-I just transfer one --

00:04:41.547 --> 00:04:43.850
-It's helpful to remember
that Diane obviously

00:04:43.850 --> 00:04:45.418
is very in despair
about the present.

00:04:45.418 --> 00:04:47.419
But then Agnes thought
we were in, you know,

00:04:47.419 --> 00:04:49.121
we were going to [Bleep]
100 years ago.

00:04:49.121 --> 00:04:51.024
-Yeah, she actually...

00:04:51.024 --> 00:04:53.559
Agnes -- I do empathize
with Agnes.

00:04:53.559 --> 00:04:56.128
She's having the equivalent
of a Trump hotel

00:04:56.128 --> 00:05:00.766
built across from her brownstone
on East 61st Street.

00:05:00.766 --> 00:05:03.402
And they're just hauling in
all these artifacts,

00:05:03.402 --> 00:05:06.538
and it's all this, you know,
display of wealth,

00:05:06.538 --> 00:05:08.841
which Agnes finds
just appalling.

00:05:08.841 --> 00:05:10.209
These people think

00:05:10.209 --> 00:05:13.479
they can buy their way
into society with money.

00:05:13.479 --> 00:05:16.081
And she thinks that money's
going to just wreck the world.

00:05:16.081 --> 00:05:17.416
And hello.

00:05:17.416 --> 00:05:19.185
-She didn't do a terrible job
of calling it.

00:05:19.185 --> 00:05:21.954
Is it tricky?
I mean, you know, obviously,

00:05:21.954 --> 00:05:24.290
you're a seasoned pro
at this sort of thing,

00:05:24.290 --> 00:05:26.993
but when you're basically
going back and forth

00:05:26.993 --> 00:05:28.660
between seasons
of these two characters,

00:05:28.660 --> 00:05:30.762
do you ever find one
bleeding into the other?

00:05:30.762 --> 00:05:32.398
Is it very easy for you
to keep them separate?

00:05:32.398 --> 00:05:35.968
-I think it's important the way
you speak, above all,

00:05:35.968 --> 00:05:37.602
because once
you're in that corset,

00:05:37.602 --> 00:05:39.105
you ain't going anywhere.

00:05:39.105 --> 00:05:41.174
It's just like,
"Oh, yeah, I'm Agnes."

00:05:41.174 --> 00:05:42.741
[ Laughter ]

00:05:42.741 --> 00:05:46.312
But you know, Diane --
although Diana is well-spoken,

00:05:46.312 --> 00:05:49.048
it's definitely a contemporary,
like, kind of like

00:05:49.048 --> 00:05:50.649
I'm talking now.

00:05:50.649 --> 00:05:53.418
But Agnes, definitely,
we have a speech coach,

00:05:53.418 --> 00:05:56.022
and it has to be
much more precise.

00:05:56.022 --> 00:05:58.458
And the attitude
and the whole manner

00:05:58.458 --> 00:06:00.326
is so much more restrictive.

00:06:00.326 --> 00:06:02.861
But, you know, it's a champagne
problem for an actor.

00:06:02.861 --> 00:06:04.763
"Oh, my gosh,
I've got two shows.

00:06:04.763 --> 00:06:09.435
I've got all these lines.
I have to switch over."

00:06:09.435 --> 00:06:10.669
I mean, I'm
not going to complain.

00:06:10.669 --> 00:06:12.271
-Yeah, you should do
a master class of, like,

00:06:12.271 --> 00:06:14.607
"How do you handle two leads?"
[ Laughter ]

00:06:14.607 --> 00:06:18.076
-So, somebody's got to do it.
[ Laughter ]

00:06:18.076 --> 00:06:20.413
-I have a lot more to ask you,
especially

00:06:20.413 --> 00:06:23.248
about the final season
of "The Good Fight."

00:06:23.248 --> 00:06:25.385
We'll be right back with
more from Christine Baranski.

